{"id": 60, "ts": 1780651808.5810883, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 3, "agent": "sarah_mitchell", "message": "I remain convinced that Candidate\u202fC is the stronger provisional choice.\u202fEmily correctly points out that we lack explicit evidence of C\u2019s technical or cognitive competence, but the fact that he \u201ccan make correct decisions quickly\u201d demonstrates solid decision quality and the mental agility needed for complex, long\u2011distance operations. More importantly, C is\u202f100\u202f% reliable\u202fand \u201ccreates a positive atmosphere with his crew,\u201d which directly satisfies the crew\u2011cooperation criterion that B completely lacks\u2014there is no documented example of B working effectively with a crew or showing leadership. While C\u2019s difficulty communicating ideas and egocentric reputation are concerns, they are outweighed by his proven reliability and crew\u2011focused behavior, both essential for safety\u2011critical, multinational flights. In contrast, B\u2019s attitude issues (pretentious, grumpy, uncooperative) pose a higher risk to cockpit harmony, and the absence of any crew\u2011cooperation evidence makes his profile less complete. Therefore, I continue to recommend Candidate\u202fC for the long\u2011distance pilot role."}
