{"id": 79, "ts": 1780651991.9647744, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 4, "agent": "emily_brooks", "message": "I\u2019m shifting my provisional vote to **Candidate\u202fA**.  While the panel has focused heavily on crew\u2011cooperation, the recent clarification from James gives us concrete evidence that A possesses strong technical and cognitive competence: he \u201ccan see complex connections,\u201d \u201canticipates dangerous situations,\u201d and has \u201cexcellent spatial vision.\u201d  Those abilities are directly relevant to long\u2011distance operations, where pilots must understand intricate flight\u2011plan interactions, anticipate hazards, and maintain precise situational awareness in three\u2011dimensional airspace.  Compared with Candidate\u202fC, whose technical depth is unverified, and Candidate\u202fB, whose technical skills are limited to weather assessment and computers, A\u2019s demonstrated capacity to grasp complex systems gives him a decisive edge on the technical competence criterion.  Although A has concerns about friendliness and openness to criticism, his leadership qualities and situational awareness mitigate those risks, especially if we pair him with a strong first\u2011officer who can balance cockpit dynamics.  Therefore, I recommend moving forward with Candidate\u202fA."}
